treatment technologies") that involves the [ combustion ](/wiki/Combustion
"Combustion") of substances contained in waste materials.  [1]  Industrial
plants for waste incineration are commonly referred to as **waste-to-energy**
facilities. Incineration and other high-temperature waste treatment systems
are described as " [ thermal treatment ](/wiki/Thermal_treatment "Thermal
treatment") ". Incineration of waste materials converts the waste into [ ash
](/wiki/Incinerator_bottom_ash "Incinerator bottom ash") , [ flue gas
](/wiki/Flue_gas "Flue gas") and heat. The ash is mostly formed by the [
inorganic ](/wiki/Inorganic "Inorganic") constituents of the waste and may
take the form of solid lumps or [ particulates
](/wiki/Atmospheric_particulate_matter "Atmospheric particulate matter")
carried by the flue gas. The flue gases must be cleaned of gaseous and
particulate pollutants before they are dispersed into the [ atmosphere
](/wiki/Atmosphere "Atmosphere") . In some cases, the heat that is generated
by incineration can be used to generate [ electric power
](/wiki/Electric_power "Electric power") .

