When a conventional spring, without stiffness variability features, is
compressed or stretched from its resting position, it exerts an opposing [
force ](/wiki/Force "Force") approximately proportional to its change in
length (this approximation breaks down for larger deflections). The _rate_ or
_spring constant_ of a spring is the change in the force it exerts, divided by
the change in [ deflection ](/wiki/Deflection_\(engineering\) "Deflection
\(engineering\)") of the spring. That is, it is the [ gradient
](/wiki/Gradient "Gradient") of the force versus deflection [ curve
](/wiki/Curve "Curve") . An [ extension ](/wiki/Tension_\(physics\) "Tension
\(physics\)") or [ compression ](/wiki/Compression_\(physical\) "Compression
\(physical\)") spring's rate is expressed in units of force divided by
distance, for example or N/m or lbf/in. A [ torsion spring
](/wiki/Torsion_spring "Torsion spring") is a spring that works by twisting;
when it is twisted about its axis by an angle, it produces a [ torque
](/wiki/Torque "Torque") proportional to the angle. A torsion spring's rate is
in units of torque divided by angle, such as [ N·m ](/wiki/Newton_metre
"Newton metre") / [ rad ](/wiki/Radian "Radian") or [ ft·lbf
](/wiki/Ft%C2%B7lbf "Ft·lbf") /degree. The inverse of spring rate is
compliance, that is: if a spring has a rate of 10 N/mm, it has a compliance of
0.1 mm/N. The stiffness (or rate) of springs in parallel is [ additive
](/wiki/Additive_map "Additive map") , as is the compliance of springs in
series.

